  <annotation ref="cb-one" text="The point 1 comma 1, where the two curves intersect."/>
  <annotation ref="cb-centroid" text="The centroid of the region at 9 twentieths comma 9 twentieths, marked in maroon. It lies on the line y equals x because the region is symmetric about that line."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-cardioid-centroid">
<title>Centroid of a Cardioid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the centroid of the object that has the shape
<m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m> and density <m>\delta = 2</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(The cardioid is shown in <xref ref="fig-cardioid-centroid"/>. Use its
symmetry to dispose of one coordinate. Work in polar coordinates with
<m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m> from <xref ref="eq-polar-area-element"/> and
<m>x = r\cos\theta</m>: compute <m>M</m> and <m>M_y</m> from
<xref ref="eq-first-moments-2d"/>. In the <m>\theta</m>-integrals, the
odd powers of cosine integrate to zero over a full period,
<m>\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^2\theta\,d\theta = \pi</m>, and
<m>\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^4\theta\,d\theta = \tfrac{3\pi}{4}</m>. Then divide
as in <xref ref="eq-com-2d"/>; what happens to <m>\delta = 2</m>?
Compare with <xref ref="fig-cardioid-centroid-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12.5) -- (15,12.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.5) -- (15,8.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.7) {by symmetry:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.2) {$M = \displaystyle\iint_R \delta\,dA =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.2) {$M_y = \displaystyle\iint_R x\,\delta\,dA =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$\bar x = \dfrac{M_y}{M} =$ \qquad centroid:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the symmetry argument, for the mass in polar coordinates, for the moment about the y axis, and for the centroid.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-centroid-video">
